  "codebook": {
    "clarification": "Code a text as credit-claiming (1) when ANY of the following is present: (a) The senator announces federal funds, grants, projects, disaster declarations, designations, or other tangible benefits flowing to their state or constituents (e.g., fire grants, airport grants, Head Start funds), even without an explicit 'I secured' statement, because announcing delivered funds is the paradigmatic credit-claiming act. (b) The senator explicitly claims personal responsibility for an action or outcome: 'I secured,' 'my amendment,' 'legislation I authored,' 'at my request,' 'following my appeal/letter,' 'I helped win.' (c) The release announces the senator's OWN legislative initiative or achievement: introducing/authoring a bill or amendment, committee or floor passage of their bill, adoption of their amendment, or inclusion of provisions they championed or authored. (d) The release claims the senator's advocacy (letters, requests, pressure) produced a favorable agency decision or outcome. Shared credit with colleagues or a delegation still counts. The claimed benefit may be actual or in-progress (e.g., a bill just introduced), as long as the senator presents it as their own effort on behalf of constituents.",
    "definition": "Credit-claiming occurs when a senator uses a press release to present themselves (or their office/delegation) as personally responsible for causing the government to do something desirable: securing federal funds, grants, projects, designations, or favorable agency decisions for constituents; authoring, sponsoring, passing, or including provisions in legislation; or successfully pressing agencies or other officials to act. The core feature is an explicit or implied claim of personal responsibility for a governmental benefit, achievement, or initiative, including advertising one's own legislative initiatives as accomplishments.",
    "negative_clarification": "Do NOT code as credit-claiming (0) when the release consists of: (a) pure statements of policy position, opinion, or reaction to events; (b) criticism of the administration or political opponents; (c) explanations of votes without any claim of responsibility for an outcome; (d) condolences, commemorations, remembrances, or ceremonies honoring OTHER people; (e) announcements of town meetings, hearings, or events without claimed deliverables; (f) advocacy, demands, requests, or oversight questioning with no achieved outcome and no claim of personal responsibility for a benefit; (g) merely 'welcoming,' 'applauding,' or 'hailing' decisions made by others without claiming to have influenced them; (h) crediting other actors (the President, colleagues, or one's party generally) rather than oneself; (i) third-party awards recognizing one's voting record (advertising positions, not claiming benefits). Note the boundary: claiming to have CAUSED a decision ('after my appeal,' 'responding to my concerns') is credit-claiming; simply praising a decision someone else made is not. Cosponsoring another senator's bill without claiming provisions is position-taking; authoring or introducing one's own bill is credit-claiming.",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "656",
        "explanation": "Kerry reacts to the collapse of the Dubai Ports deal by attacking the administration's port-security record. He takes a position and criticizes opponents but claims no personal responsibility for any benefit or outcome, so this is pure position-taking, not credit-claiming."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "2003",
        "explanation": "Clinton announces and 'hails' an Air Force decision to station a Predator squadron in New York. Although this is good news for her state, she never claims to have secured or influenced the decision; merely welcoming another actor's decision is association, not credit-claiming. This illustrates the boundary with cases like doc 2318, where the senator explicitly claims to have helped secure the outcome."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "453",
        "explanation": "Nelson explains why he voted against two Iraq withdrawal resolutions and advocates 'measurable goals.' Explaining votes and urging a policy course is position-taking; he claims no delivered benefit or legislative achievement."
      }
    ],
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "3482",
        "explanation": "Mikulski and Cardin announce a $294,980 federal SAFER grant for Allegany County firefighters. The release reinforces Mikulski's personal responsibility by noting she sits on the subcommittee that funds the program and 'fights each year to increase its federal funding' - the classic funding-announcement credit claim."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "2627",
        "explanation": "Talent announces that 'the Senate has approved his request for $1.5 million' for a Mizzou research center, explicitly framing the appropriation as his own secured funding, reinforced by university officials praising 'Senator Talent for securing the money.'"
      },
      {
        "document_id": "2318",
        "explanation": "Durbin's letter explicitly states 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review from the federal government and facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding' - a direct, first-person claim of responsibility for federal actions benefiting an Illinois project."
      }
    ]
  }
}
